     <rdf:li>NENA</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Near East</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>North Africa</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Agriculture Development</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Agriculture</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Aquaculture System</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Aquaponic</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Hydrofarm</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Farm</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">24903_0067</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">15 November 2017, Cairo, Egypt - Plants growing inside a greenhouse at Agrimatic company farm (Aquaponic) are seen during the visit of an FAO team and farmers from Egypt, Algeria  and Oman, part of the program of Farmer-to-farmer study trips.&#xA;The main objective of these farmer-to-farmer study trips is to promote and develop national potential on effective sustainable non-conventional water use practices for integrated agriculture-aquaculture production systems through the exchange of knowledge, experiences and expertise among farmers from the three studied countries (Algeria, Egypt and Oman).&#xA;Aquaponics farms developed technique of soilless agriculture. The method uses a â€œclean and greenâ€ method of growing fish and plants together in a closed system that mimics nature, where plants get their nutrition from the treated waste of the fish. Their systems achieve a high level of control over the plant environment, achieving superior growth rates, productivity, sustainability, and quality.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
   <xmpMM:History>
